lth in low concentrations but can be toxic at high levels. The dietary reference intake for copper varies across different age groups, and specific intake recommendations are outlined in dietary guidelines. \n\nDeficiency of copper is common in certain medical conditions, such as after biliopancreatic diversion and in patients undergoing hematopoietic cell transplantation, which can lead to various health issues. In infants receiving nutritional supplementation, copper levels are closely monitored to prevent toxicity. Excessive copper can lead to serious health conditions, including Wilson's disease, and may contribute to oxidative stress, potentially impacting neurodegenerative diseases.\n\nCopper is also studied for its potential role in various diseases, including cancer. In the context of parenteral nutrition, copper is considered a contaminant, and its levels should be carefully managed to avoid toxicity. Overall, copper is a crucial micronutrient that must be maintained within a balanced range to support health effectively.\",16\r\n11828,CUPROPLASMINA,\"Proteína que transporta cobre en la sangre, cuya deficiencia puede estar relacionada con la enfermedad de Wilson.\",2\r\n11891,ACETATO DE ZINC,Acetato de zinc is a treatment for Wilson's disease that blocks intestinal copper absorption and is used as a chronic maintenance therapy.,1\r\n11890,ASOCIACIÓN ESPAÑOLA DE FAMILIARES Y ENFERMOS DE WILSON,\"The Asociación Española de Familiares y Enfermos de Wilson is an organization that provides support for families and patients affected by Wilson's disease, offering resources and information through their website.\",1\r\n11872,CALCIFICACIONES ARTICULARES,Las calcificaciones articulares son depósitos de calcio en las articulaciones que pueden estar presentes en pacientes con enfermedad de Wilson.,1\r\n11873,CUCURRIA,\"La cupruria es la eliminación de cobre en la orina, que se utiliza para evaluar la presencia de la enfermedad de Wilson.\",1\r\n11874,CUPREMIA,,1\r\n1183